body: "## Context\n\nPull request 51 adopts the SDK's `scanMutationSecrets` for memory writes and imports. In seven review rounds, every round found one more spelling that slips past the scan when a credential sits inside mixed content:\n\n- a quoted key (`\"password\":\"…\"`, `'password':'…'`);\n- a value nested in an array or object (`Config: {\"password\":[\"…\"]}`, `{\"password\":{\"value\":\"…\"}}`);\n- a reference suffix, an unterminated quote, or bracket quoting.\n\nEach fix added another case to a local parser. The SDK has the same gap: `scanMutationSecrets` on 2026.11.10 returns no finding for any quoted-key form, even a flat `{\"password\":\"…\"}`, while it flags `password: …`. This is filed upstream as unbraind/pm-cli issue 1699.\n\n## Decision\n\nPull request 51 merges at its real scope: SDK transaction readers, revision guards, and the scan for unquoted assignments, reference suffixes and quoted values. Its tests cover those forms. The structural quoted-key and nested-value class is handled here instead of in more per-round patches.\n\n## Work\n\n1. When pm-cli issue 1699 ships, raise the `@unbrained/pm-cli` peer floor to that release. Delete the local mixed-content handling that the SDK rule then covers, and keep only what is still memory-specific.\n2. Add a must-refuse corpus with the quoted-key, nested-array, nested-object and XML forms above, and a must-allow corpus (`\"password\": \"\"`, `\"password\": \"${BUILD_PASSWORD}\"`, `\"password_policy\": \"rotate monthly\"`, prose that mentions a password field). Run both through `remember`, `import` and portable transfer.\n3. Until then, the README's security section states which forms are not yet refused.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Both corpora pass through every write path, and each must-refuse case fails when the scan is reverted.\n- No local regex duplicates an SDK rule.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (3)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-11-10T12:02:16Z)\n>\n> <!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n> \n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Possible Duplicate Issue(s)</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> - https://github.com/unbraind/pm-memory/issues/52\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\":\"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-10T17:18:26Z)\n>\n> Another must-refuse case for the corpus, found by the review of the final round of pull request 51 (head 717b584): environment-reference masking accepts a literal concatenated after an empty reference. In `password=${EMPTY}\"SyntheticOnly123!\"`, the quoted literal becomes the password when EMPTY is empty, yet the scan masks the whole value as a reference and admits it. Same class as the quoted-key and nested-value forms: deciding what counts as a reference belongs in the shared SDK rule, not a local parser. The corpus should also cover `${VAR}literal`, `$VAR\"literal\"` and `literal${VAR}` as must-refuse, and `${VAR}` alone as must-allow.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-10T17:55:45Z)\n>\n> Upstream status: unbraind/pm-cli pull request 1701 (for issue 1699) adds SDK recognition of quoted keys, values nested in arrays and objects, escaped literals and `<password>` elements. The env-reference concatenation case (`password=${EMPTY}\"literal\"`, `$VAR\"literal\"`, `literal${VAR}`) is not in it. It is tracked upstream as pm-cli item pm-ycgb5g, because fixing it changes the flat value grammar that also drives the repository scan. Plan: raise the peer floor and delete the local quoted/nested parsing when 1701 ships, and keep the env-reference must-refuse cases as known gaps until pm-ycgb5g ships."
